WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:05.749 align:start position:0%
 
it's really hard to tell the story  coherently of what happens to plastic  you know to watch the news and to look  at all the media coverage you would  think the debate about plastic waste has  already been resolved and won  but then if you actually look at the  numbers not only has the amount of  plastic that we're producing massively  increased it's going to increase again  significantly in the next 10 years  we are producing more plastic than ever  and it has to go somewhere so it's very  important to know what happens to this  stuff after we've finished using it  Tesco is a very large supermarket chain  it's the UK's largest by far it makes an  enormous amount of profit every year  I noticed at my local Tesco Superstore  that these white boxes has appeared with  a big sign on saying you know recycle  your soft Plastics here recycle your  wrappers your bags your crisp packets  your bread bags I wanted to find an  electronic way to follow this stuff so I  could be absolutely sure because it's  very difficult to track waste streams  around the world  so we've just seen a red unmarked Lorry  and we're gonna follow it for a while  and see where it goes  I assumed that  um all the waste collected by Tesco  would have a single outcome it would go  to one place it would either be buried  or it would be recycled what I  discovered was a much more complicated  picture than that it just is very  complicated very messy that's good and  bad  hi my name is kit I'm an investigative  reporter with Bloomberg most people have  no idea what happens to to their waste  once they put it in a in a in a bin or a  trash can and that's especially true of  plastic  it just goes away you know it's  collected by a van or it's deposited at  a recycling point and then it goes into  this hidden ecosystem that very few  people have oversight of it goes off to  sort of dirty smelly places to be sorted  and dealt with by someone else and you  don't think about it again but I was  fascinated to find out exactly what  happens to it and exactly where these  places are and where it goes  Tesco makes a big play of reuse as one  of its efforts to to help prevent  plastic pollution and by that they mean  that the Plastic Products they're  selling to Consumers they want to be  able to recycle them and turn them into  something else of equivalent so they  want to be able to reuse bottles and  packaging that they sell  I had I had three digital trackers  placed in in wrappers or bags and put  into the Tesco boxes which said they  were going to recycle them and then I  had to wait a few days to see where they  went  um and uh yeah they started to move uh  within three or four days of me dropping  them off and I could see them moving by  a truck  um one of them went to East London and  then sort of disappeared into what  looked like the banks of the Thames uh  it may have been lost it may have been  shaken loose I really don't know what  happened to it but the two other ones  took a pretty clear path which was to go  to these these Tesco Logistics centers  uh you know on the outskirts of London  and then they both headed for the same  port which is Harwich International and  from there  um they went quiet for a few hours and  the next time I saw them was when they  were in the Netherlands and heading east  [Music]  there are some kinds of plastic that are  relatively easy to recycle and those can  be sold on the open market profitably  things like clear plastic bottles are  made from something called pet and  that's good recycling material you can  reuse that over and over again clear  plastic wrap as long as it doesn't have  a label or colorants or you know heat  retardants in it is good recycling  material you can you can melt that down  and reprocess it but as anyone who goes  to the supermarket to buy food will know  hardly any of the Plastic Products that  we're sold are just clear wrap they have  labels on they will normally be coming  into contact with food and all that  means that it's not all the same stuff  it's different grades of plastic and you  can't just put all that material  together into one bulk load and then  melt it in recycle it what you'd get if  you did that is unusable terrible  quality plastic so what you need to do  is separate it and to this day the only  way to do that is to have people go  through by hand feel the material with  their fingers sometimes burn it with  fighter to work out what grade of  plastic it is and separate it into  different streams  I had no idea that there's this  incredibly expensive and time consuming  process that has to go into separating  all the different grades  they didn't stay long in the Netherlands  they passed through very quickly and  then they you know went on a German  Motorway and zipped across the country  in the space of sort of 12 to 24 hours  and finally they crossed the border into  Poland and I could see that both digital  trackers although this happened on  different days ended up at precisely the  same spot  this is the Euro key site in Chile  lagora jilanagora is a small a small  town in in West Poland It's relatively  close to the border with Germany  so we we knew we had to go and see where  Tesco's waste was being sent and we knew  the exact location it was going to so I  took a flight to Poland and I teamed up  with my colleague wojcek and uh we we  simply got in the car and drove out to  jalonagora and did a drive-by to to look  at the facility  can see  piles and piles and piles of plastic  this is an exit gate  what we found in jalonagora was a long  industrial building a warehouse it must  have been as long as a football field or  longer they were you know there were  hundreds of tons of material just  sitting out there waiting to be sent  somewhere else  mountains of it  the reason plastic's going there is that  there's a particular British run company  called Euro key  and Yorkie opened this large sorting  facility on the outskirts of shalonagora  and that's where almost all the wastes  that's coming through this Supermarket  system ends up  there are two main reasons why a lot of  plastic waste is exported the first is  that disposing of plastic in a developed  economy like the UK or Germany or the US  is expensive  if you have to landfill for example if  you have to bury the plastic because you  can't use it that costs let's say 100  pounds a ton to do in the UK but the  equivalent cost in Poland is  significantly less it might cost you 20  or 30 pounds a ton to use a landfill  facility in Poland  but there are also um lots of government  incentives that are designed to make  recycling a more attractive proposition  you get credits from the government for  exporting material for recycling  so if your choice is export to Poland or  recycle a material in the UK the  government gives you as much financial  support for exporting as it does for  doing it domestically  [Applause]  [Music]  I mean the most important exercise for  the purposes of this project was to find  out what happened to the bad plastic  because we know that some kinds of  plastic are easy to use now the problem  that the world has to Grapple with is  what happens to the bad plastic what  happens to the stuff that simply isn't  economic to use again  so one of my digital trackers was inside  a lentil puff wrapper like a snack  wrapper I was very keen to see where  that one went and uh I saw it on the  move again about 24 hours after it had  been sorted at shalonagora and it headed  east across Poland to a tiny town called  ponytover and I found out there that it  had been sent to a company called  Stellar pack  Stellar pack are an Eastern European Bag  Maker they make garbage bags essentially  sort of black sacks and things like that  and they have a facility there that can  turn soft plastic back into soft plastic  but they also have a way to dispose of  the plastic they can't use they have an  on-site incinerator which provides heat  and energy for their Factory  Plastics are made from oil  they're essentially fossil fuels so they  burn really well  they burn in a similar way because crude  oil does and so they're great material  for uh incinerating for energy  and in Poland they have a huge cement  industry the cement industry requires a  lot of heat and energy to calcify  Limestone powder and turn it into a  cement they have these gigantic Kilns  that run up to 1 500 degrees Celsius or  2000 degrees Celsius  uh and that has traditionally uh relied  on polish coal for the NG source to to  heat the Kilns  um but in recent years the cement  industry has discovered uh all this  waste material coming into Poland  and  um that it's there's an abundance of it  and that it's very very cheap  and so um it's widespread practice  across Poland now for plastic garbage  and other types of garbage to be  incinerated for energy for the cement  industry  it's actually better than free fuel  because they are paid to take it if  you're a cement maker waste brokers in  Poland will pay you a small sum per ton  to take their waste plastic and  incinerate it in your Kiln so you're  actually making money from your fuel  source it's a fantastic deal you know if  you're in the business of making cement  the tracker inside the Tesco plastic bag  stopped pinging when it arrived in  shalonagora after a few days and I  assumed it had been shredded or  destroyed or trampled on or taken and  then when I got back from Poland I was  surprised to see it its location had  changed and it gave its location as  Southern turkey you know a couple of  hundred kilometers from the Syrian  Border in an industrial estate  not all the plastic that goes to Turkey  is going to end up being dumped or  buried or incinerated illegally there  are good recycling companies in turkey  that have great reputations but  unfortunately turkey has a destination  for Waste has huge problems and a lot of  the plastic that goes to Turkey ends up  being illegally disposed of  the tracker ended up at this industrial  Estates uh a sort of a quite remote one  um  there was no sort of there was no  plastic recycling company nearby that we  could identify so we were confused as to  why it was there and uh you know we we  sent a journalist to the ground  um to drive out and visit this site what  she found outside a warehouse just just  left  in a big pile was tons and tons of waste  from plastic waste from around Europe  um the facility itself wasn't a  recycling facility it was obviously just  left there before it could be moved  somewhere else  you know I don't have any idea what  happened to the the tracker material  that went to that industrial estate in  Turkey  um I can't imagine that it was possible  to recycle it but I don't know what  happened to it after that but we know  that Tesco waste material has been found  in that very area you know for a long  period of time as recently as a few  months ago uh you know I spoke to an  activist who found a Tesco carrier bag  just left by the side of the field in a  smoldering pile of burnt plastic  obviously had been illegally dumped  foreign  [Music]  supermarket chain it's the UK's largest  by far it makes an enormous amount of  profit every year  and it also has this incredible  Logistics infrastructure in place to  make sure it's fresh bread and milk and  meat at all its stores around the UK it  has a network  and if this entity can't properly  dispose of plastic you know what hope is  there for a little cash strapped Town  Council that we want to dispose of our  plastic by the curbside  foreign  Tesco is exporting plastic is because  it's the most cost efficient solution to  their problem  so the reality is despite all the you  know the the nice words and the good  intentions that's still the vast  majority of soft plastic that goes into  the market is either going to be  incinerated or it's going to be buried  foreign  [Music]

